Yes.

9
00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:53.280
The enduring genius of PayPal's wallet model was that moving money into PayPal's ecosystem cost a substantial amount of money via card interchange.

10
00:00:53.280 --> 00:01:02.240
PayPal would try to set their pricing, historically about 2.9% plus about 30 cents, such that it was above most interchange fees they need moving money into the system.

11
00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:06.240
Then they would strongly encourage users to keep that balance within PayPal.

12
00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.720
This was natural for many casual eBay sellers.

13
00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:17.760
They were buyers as well, rather than being professional antique dealers, and leaving their internet money somewhere on the internet until the next needed money on the internet worked out fine.

14
00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:29.280
When a customer did the next transaction, paying from their PayPal balance to another PayPal user, the money moved internally at the speed and cost of a database transaction, approximately instantaneously and too cheap to meter.

15
00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:40.400
And then PayPal would try, with varying levels of success, to charge the full 2.9% plus 30 cents, this time making 290 bits of margin rather than 80 to 120 bits or so of margin.

16
00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:54.720
Supercharging of the margin possibilities by disintermediating the card ecosystems is the primary economic advantage of all that's capable of holding balances.

17
00:01:54.720 --> 00:02:06.920
I emphasize this because non-specialists routinely assume the thing that looks like a bank deposit and might actually be a bank deposit under the hood must earn revenue in the same way that bank deposits earn revenue via net interest margin.

18
00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:07.880
It does.

19
00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:13.320
And in high interest environments like the one we're currently living in, at least in the US, this is lucrative.

20
00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:19.880
